## 2. The upper bound

We use the Chow-valued Faber–Zagier relations.

Define
\[
A(T)=\sum_{n\ge0}\frac{(6n)!}{(3n)!(2n)!}T^n,\qquad
B(T)=\sum_{n\ge0}\frac{(6n)!}{(3n)!(2n)!}
                  \frac{6n+1}{6n-1}T^n,
\]
and
\[
C_{3i}=T^iA,\qquad C_{3i+1}=T^iB.
\]
For a series \(F\), put
\[
\{F\}=\sum_{n\ge0}[F]_{T^n}K_nT^n.
\]
The linear operation \(\mathsf K\) is defined on monomials by
\[
\mathsf K(K_{e_1}\cdots K_{e_m})
=
\sum_{\tau\in S_m}
\prod_{\text{cycles }c\text{ of }\tau}
\kappa_{\sum_{i\in c}e_i},
\qquad \kappa_0=48.
\]

The Faber–Zagier theorem states that
\[
\left[
\mathsf K\left(
 \exp(\{1-A\})\prod_{s\in\sigma}\{C_s\}
\right)
\right]_{T^r}=0
\tag{3}
\]
whenever

* no part of \(\sigma\) is \(2\bmod3\);
* \(3r\ge26+|\sigma|\);
* \(25\equiv r+|\sigma|+1\pmod2\).

Take all such relations with \(r\le12\), and multiply each by all \(\kappa\)-monomials of degree \(12-r\). Their numbers are
\[
\begin{array}{c|rrrr|r}
r&9&10&11&12&\text{total}\\ \hline
\text{degree-12 relations}&3&10&13&33&59.
\end{array}
\]
Expressed in the \(77\) degree-\(12\) monomials, these relations have rank \(33\) modulo \(1000000007\), as checked below. Therefore they contain \(33\) linearly independent rational relations, and
\[
\dim_{\mathbb Q}R^{12}(\mathcal M_{25})
\le77-33=44.
